  <section class="summary" aria-labelledby="sum">
    <h2 id="sum">The 30 second version</h2>
    <div><span class="k">Problem</span><p>Rewards come from eleven systems and show up wherever each one put them, so the player has no single place to see what he has earned.</p></div>
    <div><span class="k">Decision</span><p>Five buckets ordered by what the player can act on answer those questions in the order he asks them.</p></div>
    <div><span class="k">The one metric that matters</span><p>Claim rate. Rewards claimed out of rewards made claimable.</p></div>
    <div><span class="k">What I learned</span><p>On a rewards screen, a number and its bar disagreeing is the fastest way to lose a player’s trust.</p></div>
  </section>

  <div class="layout">
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents">
      <span class="k">Contents</span>
      <a href="#problem">Problem understanding</a>
      <a href="#structure">Structure</a>
      <a href="#userflow">User flow</a>
      <a href="#wireframes">Wireframes</a>
      <a href="#hifi">High fidelity</a>
      <a href="#rationale">Design rationale</a>
      <a href="#system">System</a>
      <a href="#metrics">Success metrics</a>
      <a href="#learned">What I learned</a>
    </nav>
    <button type="button" class="tocpill" popovertarget="tocsheet" aria-label="Contents"><span class="k">Contents</span><b>Problem understanding</b><i aria-hidden="true">1 / 9</i><span class="chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
    <div id="tocsheet" class="tocsheet" popover><span class="k">Contents</span></div>
    <div class="dockfade" aria-hidden="true"></div><nav class="dock" aria-label="Contents"><button type="button" class="dkb" data-go="-1" aria-label="Previous section"><svg aria-hidden="true" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M15 18l-6-6 6-6"/></svg></button><div class="dkm"><div class="dkt"><b></b><i></i></div><div class="dks"></div></div><a class="dkn" href="#"><small>NEXT</small><span></span></a><button type="button" class="dkb" data-go="1" aria-label="Next section"><svg aria-hidden="true" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></button></nav>

    <article>
      <section id="problem" class="lt wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">01 · Problem understanding</p>
          <h2>Rewards are everywhere and nowhere</h2>
          <p class="lede">Eleven reward sources, each surfaced by the system that produced it. The player never gets one place to see what is his.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cards" style="--n:2;margin-top:48px;background:none">
          <div style="background:none;padding:0">
            <span class="k">The problems as stated</span>
            <div class="plist">
              <div><span class="n">01</span><p>Players miss rewards they are eligible for.</p></div>
              <div><span class="n">02</span><p>Some rewards stay unclaimed even after they are earned.</p></div>
              <div><span class="n">03</span><p>Players do not understand what they are working towards.</p></div>
              <div><span class="n">04</span><p>The experience feels fragmented.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div style="background:none;padding:0">
            <span class="k">What sits underneath</span>
            <div class="under">
              <p class="big">All four come from one decision. Rewards are organised by the system that generated them, not by what the player can do about them.</p>
              <p>The player never asks what the mission system gave him. He asks four questions, and no screen answers any of them.</p>
              <ul class="qs"><li>What do I have</li><li>What can I take right now</li><li>What am I close to</li><li>What could I start</li></ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="cards" style="--n:3;margin-top:48px">
          <div><span class="lbl">What I saw in the market</span><p>I looked at reward and loyalty screens across banking, travel, food and casual games. Almost every one still organises by source or by tab, or shrinks every programme into an icon grid. The grid looks tidy and rebuilds the eleven doors in a smaller space.</p><p class="hl">Games get two things right that I kept. The action on each row changes with its state, Claim or Go. And time is loud, a live countdown instead of a quiet date.</p></div>
          <div><span class="lbl">Primary user</span><h3>The active mobile player</h3><p>Opens most days, plays short sessions, earns across several sources at once without tracking any of them. Not disengaged, uninformed. He would claim more if he knew what was waiting.</p></div>
          <div><span class="lbl">Secondary user</span><h3>The returning player</h3><p>Back after a gap. Needs to see what survived, what expired, and what is worth starting again. The reason History shows expired rewards instead of hiding them.</p></div>
        </div>
        <span class="k sub">How I ranked the five jobs</span>
        <div class="cards" style="--n:5">
          <div><span class="num">1</span><h3>Claim with minimal effort</h3><p>Unclaimed value is the only problem that costs the player something real. Every claimable reward is on the first screen with its button on the card.</p></div>
          <div><span class="num">2</span><h3>Discover what is available</h3><p>A reward nobody sees is the same as no reward. Ready, Expiring and Available are all visible in one scroll.</p></div>
          <div><span class="num">3</span><h3>Understand progress</h3><p>Steps as counts and a percentage, one next step line, a Progress screen grouped by when things end.</p></div>
          <div><span class="num">4</span><h3>Stay motivated</h3><p>The consequence of the three above, plus one deliberate moment at claim. Not decoration on top.</p></div>
          <div><span class="num">5</span><h3>Track history</h3><p>Lowest frequency job, so it gets its own screen and one row on Home rather than permanent space.</p></div>
        </div>
        <details class="fold" open><summary><span class="k sub">Assumptions</span></summary>
        <div class="cards" style="--n:4">
          <div><h3 class="g">One currency</h3><p>Reward points, plus occasional grants like entries or items. A single balance at the top means something.</p></div>
          <div><h3 class="g">Every reward has a state</h3><p>Most also carry an expiry. State decides what the player can do, expiry decides how soon.</p></div>
          <div><h3 class="g">New types keep arriving</h3><p>The structure has to absorb the twelfth and thirteenth reward type without a redesign.</p></div>
          <div><h3 class="g">Two ways in</h3><p>A Rewards tab and notifications. The rest of the app stays untouched, per the constraint.</p></div>
        </div></details>
      </section>

      <section id="structure" class="wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">02 · Structure</p>
          <h2>Five buckets, ordered by what the player can do</h2>
          <p class="lede">Every one of the eleven sources becomes a card inside these five. The source turns into a label on the card, not a place the player has to go.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cards bk" style="--n:5;margin-top:48px">
          <div><span class="id"><i class="dot w"></i>01</span><h3>Ready to claim</h3><p>What can I take right now</p><span class="act w">Act now, no effort</span></div>
          <div><span class="id"><i class="dot c"></i>02</span><h3>Expiring soon</h3><p>What am I about to lose</p><span class="act c">Act now, time bound</span></div>
          <div><span class="id"><i class="dot m"></i>03</span><h3>In progress</h3><p>What am I close to</p><span class="act" style="color:#C9C9D1">Keep going</span></div>
          <div><span class="id"><i class="dot"></i>04</span><h3>Available</h3><p>What could I start</p><span class="act">Choose</span></div>
          <div><span class="id"><i class="dot"></i>05</span><h3>History</h3><p>What already happened</p><span class="act">Look back</span></div>
        </div>
        <span class="k sub">A reward moves between buckets as its state changes</span>
        <div class="moves">
          <div><b>Daily login</b><ol class="chain"><li><i class="dot"></i>Available</li><li><i class="dot w"></i>Ready to claim</li><li><i class="dot"></i>History</li></ol></div>
          <div><b>Weekly mission</b><ol class="chain"><li><i class="dot"></i>Available</li><li><i class="dot m"></i>In progress</li><li><i class="dot w"></i>Ready to claim</li><li><i class="dot"></i>History</li></ol></div>
          <div><b>Tournament reward</b><ol class="chain"><li><i class="dot w"></i>Ready to claim</li><li><i class="dot c"></i>Expiring soon</li><li><i class="dot"></i>History</li></ol></div>
        </div>
        <span class="k sub">Five rules that hold across all four screens</span>
        <div class="cards" style="--n:5">
          <div><span class="num">01</span><h3>One reward, one bucket</h3><p>If a reward fits two, the more urgent one wins. Expiring soon, then Ready, then In progress, then Available.</p></div>
          <div><span class="num">02</span><h3>Expiring means under 24 hours</h3><p>Ready or unfinished. The card keeps its own button, Claim or Go, and gains a live countdown.</p></div>
          <div><span class="num">03</span><h3>Home shows three per bucket</h3><p>Ready expands in place with Show more and Claim all. In progress and Available continue on Progress. History continues on History.</p></div>
          <div><span class="num">04</span><h3>Body opens, button acts</h3><p>Tapping a card always opens Detail. The button always does what the state allows. Same on every screen.</p></div>
          <div><span class="num">05</span><h3>No dead ends</h3><p>Detail always offers Start, Continue or Claim. Claiming returns to Home with the next nearest reward already showing.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p class="goldline" style="margin-top:24px;max-width:34em">The same reward shows the same numbers everywhere. Play 5 hands is 3 of 5 hands at 60 percent on Home, on Progress and on Detail, and every claimed reward in History has already left Home.</p>
        <span class="k sub">How the four screens divide the work</span>
        <div class="cards" style="--n:4">
          <div><h3 class="g">Home</h3><p>All five buckets in one scroll, three per bucket, in the order above. The answer screen.</p></div>
          <div><h3 class="g">Reward Detail</h3><p>Opens from any card, from any screen. Exact steps left, expiry, and a button that always does something.</p></div>
          <div><h3 class="g">Progress</h3><p>Everything not yet claimable, grouped by today, this week and long term. In progress first, then available.</p></div>
          <div><h3 class="g">History</h3><p>Expands History. Claimed and expired, with a running balance.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="userflow" class="lt wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">03 · User flow</p>
          <h2>Every path ends in a claim or a return to Home</h2>
          <p class="lede">Two ways to claim. Inline from the card for simple grants, through Detail for anything with conditions.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="darkcard flowpic" style="margin-top:40px" tabindex="0" aria-label="User flow diagram, scrolls sideways on small screens"><img src="img/03-flow.webp" width="1600" height="833" loading="lazy" alt="User flow. Entry: Rewards tab, Home widget or profile, and Reward notification all lead to Home (balance, next step, all five buckets). From Home the player goes to Claim from the card (simple grants, one tap), Reward Detail (card body, or the next step line), Progress (see all, or the tier track) or History (history row, or the balance). Progress opens Detail with Tap a row; History opens it read only. Claim from the card and Claim on Detail both lead to Claim success: balance counts up and the nearest reward surfaces so there is no dead end, then back to Home. An Expiry notification lands on the reward itself and returns to Home, nothing to dismiss."></figure>
        <div class="cards" style="--n:3;margin-top:24px">
          <div><h3>Back always returns to Home</h3><p>Home is the only hub. No screen links sideways except into Detail.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Detail is reachable from three screens</h3><p>Home, Progress and History all open the same Detail, read only from History.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Expiry skips the hub</h3><p>A reward about to expire opens straight on itself. Saving a tap is the point of the notification.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="wireframes" class="lt wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">04 · Wireframes</p>
          <h2>Structure before surface</h2>
          <p class="lede">Four screens at full scroll length. Blue numbers are entry points, matched in the Entered from box under each screen. Purple boxes are structural decisions. The high fidelity that follows changes the surface, not the structure.</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="wf" style="margin-top:40px">
          <li><b>Home</b><img src="img/04-wf-home.webp" width="560" height="1926" loading="lazy" alt="Home wireframe in greyscale: balance 12,480 with Silver tier, Ready to claim with Claim all, Expiring soon, In progress, Available and a History row, with purple notes on the structural decisions"></li>
          <li><b>Reward Detail</b><img src="img/04-wf-detail.webp" width="560" height="1414" loading="lazy" alt="Reward Detail wireframe for Play 5 hands: +500 points, What's left with three of five hands done, expiry Ends Sunday 28 Sep, terms, and a Continue playing button"></li>
          <li><b>Progress</b><img src="img/04-wf-progress.webp" width="560" height="2130" loading="lazy" alt="Progress wireframe: 5 in progress, 2 available, 2,600 points in play, chips for Today, This week and Long term, a seven day streak, and sections for today, this week and long term"></li>
          <li><b>History</b><img src="img/04-wf-history.webp" width="560" height="1474" loading="lazy" alt="History wireframe: lifetime claimed, 48 rewards claimed, 6 expired, filter chips, and claimed and expired rewards grouped by day with the balance after each day"></li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="hifi" class="lt wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">05 · High fidelity</p>
          <h2>The four screens</h2>
          <p class="lede">Same structure as the wireframes, surfaced. One card component in six states renders every reward on every screen. Gold is value, white is action, coral is time, and each reward type carries its own tinted icon.</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="hf" style="margin-top:48px">
          <li><b>Home</b><span>All five buckets in one scroll</span><div class="iph lt noisl"><div class="sc" tabindex="0" aria-label="Home screen, scrolls inside the frame"><img src="img/05-home.webp" width="560" height="2782" loading="lazy" alt="Home screen: balance 12,480 points at Silver, 1,520 points to Gold, Ready to claim with three cards and Claim all, a Referral bonus expiring in 04:12:57 with a coral edge, In progress, Available and History"></div><div class="navfix" aria-hidden="true" style="aspect-ratio:560/152;background-image:url(img/05-home.webp)"></div></div></li>
          <li><b>Reward Detail</b><span>Exact steps left, always one action</span><div class="iph lt noisl"><div class="sc" tabindex="0" aria-label="Reward Detail screen, scrolls inside the frame"><img src="img/05-detail.webp" width="560" height="1492" loading="lazy" alt="Reward Detail for Play 5 hands: +500 points, in progress, What's left 3 of 5 hands at 60 percent, ends Sunday 28 Sep, and a Continue playing button"></div><div class="navfix" aria-hidden="true" style="aspect-ratio:560/165;background-image:url(img/05-detail.webp)"></div></div></li>
          <li><b>Progress</b><span>Today, this week, long term</span><div class="iph lt noisl"><div class="sc" tabindex="0" aria-label="Progress screen, scrolls inside the frame"><img src="img/05-progress.webp" width="560" height="2582" loading="lazy" alt="Progress screen: 5 in progress, 2 available, 2,600 points in play, a daily streak on day 4 of 7, then this week's missions with bars and the long term Silver to Gold track"></div><div class="navfix" aria-hidden="true" style="aspect-ratio:560/153;background-image:url(img/05-progress.webp)"></div></div></li>
          <li><b>History</b><span>Claimed and expired, running balance</span><div class="iph lt noisl"><div class="sc" tabindex="0" aria-label="History screen, scrolls inside the frame"><img src="img/05-history.webp" width="560" height="1737" loading="lazy" alt="History screen: lifetime claimed 48,920 points, claimed rewards by day with the balance after each day, and an expired Friday promo with its value struck through"></div><div class="navfix" aria-hidden="true" style="aspect-ratio:560/146;background-image:url(img/05-history.webp)"></div></div></li>
        </ul>
        <div class="proto-note">
          <h3>The prototype</h3>
          <p>Claim swaps the card to its claimed state in place, which is the moment this screen exists for. Home links out to Detail, Progress and History, and every screen returns to Home. I scoped the concept to four screens, so only Play 5 hands opens a Detail page; in the product every card opens its own.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="protowrap" id="prototype" style="margin-top:40px;scroll-margin-top:96px"><iframe class="proto" title="Unified Reward Centre Figma prototype" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen src="https://www.figma.com/embed?embed_host=share&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.figma.com%2Fproto%2FLqPzEwyFiliPAkHO5d0i2n%2FUnified-Reward-Centre%3Fnode-id%3D99-6354%26starting-point-node-id%3D99%253A6354%26scaling%3Dscale-down%26content-scaling%3Dfixed"></iframe><div class="protowait"><img src="img/05-home.webp" width="560" height="2782" alt="" loading="lazy" style="height:240px;object-fit:cover;object-position:top"><p>Loading the prototype</p><a href="https://www.figma.com/proto/LqPzEwyFiliPAkHO5d0i2n/Unified-Reward-Centre?node-id=99-6354&amp;starting-point-node-id=99%3A6354" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Figma</a></div><button type="button" class="protogo"><span>Tap to try the prototype</span></button><button type="button" class="protodone">Close prototype</button></figure>
