      </section>

      <section id="rationale" class="lt wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">06 · Design rationale</p>
          <h2>Why it is built this way</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="cards" style="--n:2;margin-top:40px">
          <div><h3>Why this structure</h3><p>The eleven sources were organised around the systems that produced them. Players think in four questions instead. Five buckets ordered by what the player can act on answer those questions in the order he asks them.</p><p>None of the reward screens I looked at order by state. They order by source or by tab. That makes this the move that separates the screen, not a safe default.</p></div>
          <div><h3>How it reduces complexity</h3><p>One destination replaces eleven. One card component renders every reward type, so the pattern is learned once and read everywhere.</p><p>Section order never changes, and Expiring soon only appears when something is genuinely at risk. The screen stays predictable without going stale.</p></div>
          <div><h3>How the player knows what is next</h3><p>A single next step line under the balance names one action. In progress shows remaining steps as steps, three of five hands, not a percentage.</p><p>Claiming never ends in a dead end. The balance counts up and the nearest reward surfaces. Available is ranked by value against effort, so choosing is not research.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Trade offs</h3>
            <div class="trades">
              <div><h4>Clarity over decoration</h4><p>One moment celebrates, the claim. Everything else stays still, because celebration that fires constantly stops registering within a week.</p></div>
              <div><h4>Detail on its own screen</h4><p>One extra tap on conditional rewards, in exchange for a Home that stays scannable.</p></div>
              <div><h4>Expired stays visible</h4><p>It looks worse in a screenshot. It is the honest answer, and the strongest reason to open the screen tomorrow.</p></div>
              <div><h4>Available lives in Progress</h4><p>Stays inside the four screen scope, without a fifth screen just for discovery.</p></div>
              <div><h4>Sections, not tabs, on Progress</h4><p>Tabs would hide two thirds of what the player is working towards. The chips jump between horizons instead of filtering them.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="system" class="lt wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">07 · System</p>
          <h2>One reward object, six states, one card</h2>
          <p class="lede">Every reward the product will ever have is the same object. What changes is its state, and the state decides which card it becomes, which bucket it sits in, and what its button says.</p>
        </div>
        <span class="k sub">What a reward is, in plain language</span>
        <div class="cards" style="--n:6">
          <div><h3 class="g">Type</h3><p>Daily login, mission, tournament, cashback, referral, milestone, promo. Decides the glyph and its tint.</p></div>
          <div><h3 class="g">Source</h3><p>Where it came from. Becomes the small label on the card, never a separate destination.</p></div>
          <div><h3 class="g">State</h3><p>Available, in progress, ready, expiring, claimed, expired. Decides the bucket, the card and the button.</p></div>
          <div><h3 class="g">Value</h3><p>Points, an item or an entry. Set in Unbounded, gold, the hero of the card.</p></div>
          <div><h3 class="g">Progress</h3><p>Optional. 3 of 5, 640 of 1,000. Drives the bar and the percentage.</p></div>
          <div><h3 class="g">Expiry</h3><p>Optional. Under 24 hours it switches the card to Expiring and starts the countdown.</p></div>
        </div>
        <span class="k sub">State decides everything on the card</span>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">Bucket on Home</th><th scope="col">Button</th><th scope="col">What is loud</th><th scope="col">Colour</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Available</td><td data-l="Bucket on Home">Available</td><td data-l="Button">Start</td><td data-l="What is loud">Value</td><td data-l="Colour">Outline only</td></tr>
            <tr><td>In progress</td><td data-l="Bucket on Home">In progress</td><td data-l="Button">Go</td><td data-l="What is loud">Bar and what you will earn</td><td data-l="Colour">White bar, gold value</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ready</td><td data-l="Bucket on Home">Ready to claim</td><td data-l="Button">Claim</td><td data-l="What is loud">Value, lit</td><td data-l="Colour">White button, gold bloom</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Expiring</td><td data-l="Bucket on Home">Expiring soon</td><td data-l="Button">Claim or Go</td><td data-l="What is loud">Countdown</td><td data-l="Colour">Coral edge and pill</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Claimed</td><td data-l="Bucket on Home">History</td><td data-l="Button">None</td><td data-l="What is loud">Value and Claimed</td><td data-l="Colour">Gold, single line</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Expired</td><td data-l="Bucket on Home">History</td><td data-l="Button">None</td><td data-l="What is loud">Struck value</td><td data-l="Colour">Grey, single line</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <span class="k sub">Stress test, a reward type that does not exist yet</span>
        <div class="cards" style="--n:2">
          <div><h3>Season Pass, a 30 day ladder of 20 tiers, each tier paying out separately</h3><p>Type: season. Source: Season Pass. Value per tier. Progress: tier 6 of 20. Expiry: end of season. One glyph and one tint token added. Each tier is a card. Unlocked tiers sit in Ready to claim, the next one in In progress, the rest in Available. Nothing on the four screens changes.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Physical prize, a headset shipped to your door</h3><p>Type: item. Value: the item. Claim opens an address step on Detail instead of counting the balance up. The card, the bucket and the button are unchanged. Only Detail grows a step, which it already knows how to do.</p></div>
        </div>
        <details class="fold" open><summary><span class="k sub">Foundations in this file</span></summary>
        <div class="cards" style="--n:4">
          <div><h3 class="g">54 semantic colour tokens</h3><p>Aliased to 58 primitives. Four families: surface, text, value and action, status. Plus tier metals, type tints and success. 25 typography tokens on top.</p></div>
          <div><h3 class="g">107 text styles</h3><p>Unbounded for numbers only, Albert Sans for everything else. Eleven semantic roles on top of the size scales.</p></div>
          <div><h3 class="g">3 component sets</h3><p>Reward Card in six states, Progress bar in seven percent variants, Reward Icon with eleven glyphs on an instance swap.</p></div>
          <div><h3 class="g">1 rule for colour</h3><p>Gold is value, white is action, coral is time, accents live in a 36px tile or a chip and nowhere else.</p></div>
        </div></details>
      </section>

      <section id="metrics" class="lt wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">08 · Success metrics</p>
          <h2>How I would know it worked</h2>
          <p class="lede">Five numbers. Each one maps back to a problem named on board 01.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cards met" style="--n:5;margin-top:40px">
          <div><span class="mid">01</span><span class="tag">↑ Up</span><h3>Claim rate</h3><p>Rewards claimed out of rewards made claimable.</p><p class="hit">Answers unclaimed rewards directly.</p></div>
          <div><span class="mid">02</span><span class="tag">↓ Down</span><h3>Expiry rate</h3><p>Share of rewards that expire unclaimed.</p><p class="hit">The honest counterweight to claim rate.</p></div>
          <div><span class="mid">03</span><span class="tag">↓ Down</span><h3>Taps to claim</h3><p>From opening the Reward Centre to a claim.</p><p class="hit">Two taps for a simple grant is the target.</p></div>
          <div><span class="mid">04</span><span class="tag">↑ Up</span><h3>Completion rate</h3><p>Started rewards that get finished.</p><p class="hit">Tests whether players know what they are working towards.</p></div>
          <div><span class="mid">05</span><span class="tag">↑ Up</span><h3>D7 retention</h3><p>Players who claimed at least once, against those who never did.</p><p class="hit">Whether the screen changes behaviour or only reports it.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="guard"><span class="k">Guardrail I would watch</span><p>Claim rate rising while session length drops. That would mean players open the centre, claim and leave, which is the opposite of motivation.</p></div>
        <details class="fold" open><summary><span class="k sub">Notes</span></summary>
        <div class="cards" style="--n:3">
          <div><p>Scoped to mobile and four screens, with the rest of the app left untouched.</p></div>
          <div><p>Wireframes are greyscale on purpose, so structure is judged before colour.</p></div>
          <div><p>Reward names, values and times are illustrative, chosen to be believable rather than exact.</p></div>
        </div></details>
      </section>

      <section id="learned" class="lt wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">09 · What I learned</p>
          <h2>Where the first answer was wrong</h2>
          <p class="lede">Four decisions that changed during the build, and why. Then what I would test first with real players.</p>
        </div>
        <span class="k sub">Decisions that changed</span>
        <div class="cards" style="--n:2">
          <div><span class="num">01</span><h3>Sort by what you can do, not where it came from</h3><p>The obvious structure was a tab per reward source: login, missions, tournaments, referrals. I rejected it because the next reward type the product adds would need a new tab. Sorted by state, ready, expiring, in progress and available, a new type simply arrives as a card.</p></div>
          <div><span class="num">02</span><h3>Gold was doing two jobs</h3><p>In the first pass, in progress and claimed rewards both sat on gold, so a half finished mission looked like points already earned. I split the meanings: gold is value, white is action, coral is time. Because every colour was a token, moving action from green to white took four token changes, not a screen edit.</p></div>
          <div><span class="num">03</span><h3>A bar that disagreed with its number</h3><p>Every progress bar rendered at 62 percent, whatever its label said, because width was set by resizing and instances overrode it. I rebuilt the bar as a component that fills by layout instead. On a rewards screen, a number and its bar disagreeing is the fastest way to lose a player’s trust.</p></div>
          <div><span class="num">04</span><h3>A summary that contradicted the screen</h3><p>Points in play read 2,300 while the cards directly under it added up to 2,600, and the counts beside it were off too. Now every summary number gets checked against the cards it summarises before I call a screen done.</p></div>
        </div>
        <span class="k sub">What I would test first</span>
        <div class="cards" style="--n:3">
          <div><span class="num">A</span><h3>Time to first claim</h3><p>From opening the Reward Centre to the first claim. The clearest read on whether leading with Ready to claim actually works.</p></div>
          <div><span class="num">B</span><h3>Is coral read as today</h3><p>Show the Expiring card with its countdown hidden and ask what it means. If players do not read coral as act today, the rule needs a word, not just a colour.</p></div>
          <div><span class="num">C</span><h3>Rewards lost to expiry</h3><p>The share of rewards that expire unclaimed, before and after. It is the loss players feel most, and History now makes it visible.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>

